tomer was not visibly inebriated at the time of the sale, and the sale was not made to someone who was intoxicated or inebriated.\n\n4. The accident occurred after the sale, and the customer was intoxicated from drinking the wine. The \"blue law\" prohibiting Sunday liquor sales does not apply here because the sale was made on Sunday, and the customer was not a guest in the hotel, motel, or inn.\n\nGiven these points, the primary issue is whether the sale of the wine was in violation of the \"blue law,\" which it was not. The accident occurred after the sale, and the customer's intoxication was a result of the wine consumption, not the sale itself.\n\n**Answer: C**", "output_sha256": "cec91bd95cd53fecec0703568dc1ac5c12c1e72e4f3c6e3c5350794df88adb0e", "gold": "C", "ts": 1790126007.0302777}
