petuities because it is limited to his lifetime. This is correct. The Rule against Perpetuities generally applies to restrictions on alienation lasting more than a certain period, but the lawyer's right of first refusal is limited to his lifetime, which is not a perpetual restriction.\n\nC. The lawyer will lose because the rights of first refusal are unreasonable restraints on alienation. This is incorrect because the rights of first refusal are not unreasonable; they are a standard clause in real estate transactions.\n\nD. The lawyer will lose because the rights of first refusal relate only to land that is not conveyed by deed. This is incorrect because the rights of first refusal are related to the land conveyed by deed, not the land that is not conveyed.\n\nAnswer: B", "output_sha256": "5430c6ef9421f657984dc11b9e219fdb2586e74db74bb80109ac8508dba9066e", "gold": "B", "ts": 1790126015.5540912}
