ated equally, regardless of race, gender, or other characteristics.\n\nGiven that the requirement is based on prior experience (or lack thereof), it could be seen as a form of discrimination based on prior status, which might align more closely with the equal protection clause. However, the requirement is also procedural and could be seen as a due process issue if it unfairly burdens new entrants.\n\nThe most probable resolution would likely be to hold the authenticating requirement unconstitutional as violative of the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, as it affects the procedural fairness of the election process.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "ca9e7e40318c4078ed3bfef8c63426fd9152ee4517ececceef1cb7e55d11a8d2", "gold": "D", "ts": 1790126029.138419}
