out renegotiating the contract terms rather than repudiating it.\n\nC. $75,000 only, because that was the contract price.\n- The contractor did not explicitly agree to the $75,000 price. The owner did not accept the $80,000 price, and the contractor did not explicitly agree to the $75,000 price either. The contractor's actions suggest a renegotiation rather than a simple acceptance of the original price.\n\nD. $80,000, because the contractor reasonably relied to his detriment on the electrician's bid in formulating his job estimate.\n- The contractor did not rely on the electrician's bid in formulating his job estimate. The contractor used the electrician's bid to determine the overall cost of the job, but the contractor did not explicitly agree to the $80,000 price. The contractor's actions suggest a renegotiation rather than a simple reliance on the electrician's bid.\n\nGiven the context, the most plausible answer is:\n\nAnswer: C", "output_sha256": "d8ae817e3737869fceaf7dfd40a949648cfef43624498ffb487c0f7935e5830d", "gold": "C", "ts": 1790126066.8669431}
