n\nC. The no-alcohol term was functionally a penalty clause and therefore was unenforceable.\n- This option suggests that the no-alcohol term was intended as a penalty, which would make it unenforceable. However, the term was a condition of the contract, not a penalty, and thus this argument does not hold.\n\nD. The no-alcohol term would be interpreted as a promise and not a condition.\n- This option suggests that the no-alcohol term was a promise rather than a condition. If interpreted as a promise, the architect would have to adhere to it, and the developer's refusal to accept the plans could be seen as a breach of the architect's promise to abstain from alcohol.\n\nGiven the context, the most compelling argument is that the no-alcohol term was interpreted as a promise rather than a condition.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "3e5775e81ae90f4fd98577f6ee2f5bbe9c466d60dfd14d55a41d38239f4bab70", "gold": "B", "ts": 1790126071.6466773}
