ption:\n\nA. No, because most stolen boats remain within the state in which they were stolen.\n- This option is incorrect because the primary issue is the transportation of stolen boats, not their location within the state.\n\nB. No, because the registration of boats is a matter reserved to the states by the Tenth Amendment.\n- This option is incorrect because the Tenth Amendment reserves powers not delegated to the federal government to the states or the people. The power to regulate commerce is a federal power, not reserved to the states.\n\nC. Yes, because Congress could determine that the transportation of stolen boats affects interstate commerce.\n- This option is correct because the transportation of stolen boats across state lines clearly affects interstate commerce, which is within Congress's power under the Commerce Clause.\n\nD. Yes, because Congress has the power to regulate property for the general welfare.\n- This option is incorrect because the power to regulate interstate commerce is specifically mentioned in the Constitution, not the general welfare.\n\nAnswer: C", "output_sha256": "d6c7cc56a88a3e0a5ee2bbafb395a63d79a194309fd4b5f9c5e5030deb728b49", "gold": "C", "ts": 1790128738.9250622}
