.01-second delay due to CPU processing.\n\nTo find the effective data rate, we need to account for the CPU delay. The effective data rate is the total bytes sent per second minus the CPU delay per second.\n\nTotal bytes sent per second by the network: 1,000,000 bytes/second\nCPU delay per second: 1,000 packets * 0.01 seconds/packet = 100 seconds/second\n\nEffective data rate: 1,000,000 bytes/second - 100 bytes/second = 999,900 bytes/second\n\nHowever, this calculation assumes that the CPU delay is the only factor affecting the effective rate. In reality, the network capacity is also a limiting factor. Since the network can only handle 1,000 packets per second, and each packet is 1,000 bytes, the maximum data rate is limited by the network capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum rate at which one computer can send data to another is approximately 1,000,000 bytes/second, considering the network capacity.\n\nAnswer: A", "output_sha256": "36f02e26ed7f00a0997329584a8c555b327ad61a4f033091840a7ef61cbfa1c7", "gold": "C", "ts": 1790128798.2647774}
