program does not have any known errors. However, this does not rule out the possibility of undiscovered bugs.\n\nD. Every method in the program may safely be used in other programs.\n- Testing a program extensively for errors does not provide enough information to conclude that every method is safe to use in other programs. Bugs can be program-specific and may not manifest during extensive testing.\n\nConsidering the above analysis, the most reasonable conclusion is:\n\nAnswer: C", "output_sha256": "8ab146ca83bbbd91fcd9ac255e14bc1d92240eb554e5b1bd420a985447cd2379", "gold": "C", "ts": 1790128880.744638}
{"id": "mmlu/professional_law/1304", "group": "mmlu/professional_law/1304", "source": "mmlu", "kind": "mcq", "tier": "local", "model": "qwen2.5:3b-instruct", "correct": false, "y_error": 1, "features": {"req_tokens_log": 5.605802066295998, "req_messages": 1.0, "req_structured": 0.0, "req_tool": 0.0, "req_code": 0.0, "req_length": 0.0, "req_has_system": 0.0, "intent": "mcq", "data_class": "Public", "ret_top_sim": null, "ret_margin": null, "ret_coverage": null, "ret_verifier": null, "ret_sources": null, "ret_age_days_log": null, "ret_mismatch": null, "gen_mean_logprob": -0.3184297949115767, "gen_min_logprob": -1.7521659135818481, "gen_p10_logprob": -0.9491147398948669, "gen_mean_entropy": 0.6127442568343563, "gen_mean_margin": 4.046199490505552, "gen_frac_low_conf": 0.17870722433460076, "gen_stop_length": 0.0, "gen_out_tokens_log": 5.579729825986222, "gen_schema_valid": null, "gen_refusal": 0.0, "gen_hedge_rate": 0.0, "gen_truncated_ctx": 0.0, "gen_answer_marker": 1.0, "gen_self_contradiction": 0.0, "sys_queue_depth": 0.0, "sys_ttft_log": 4.4930043555034604, "sys_mem_pressure": 0.13876598669842954}, "prompt_tokens": 271, "completion_tokens": 264, "ttft_ms": 88.38960100001714, "total_ms": 4072.468681000032, "finish_reason": "stop", "output": "To determine which party would ultimately prevail, we need to consider the priority of the conveyances under a notice-type statute. In such a statute, the priority of conveyances is determined by the date of recording, not the date of the conveyance itself. Therefore, the party who records their deed last will have the highest priority.\n\nHere's the sequence of conveyances and their dates:\n1. The woman conveys to the man (recorded on February 1).\n2. The man conveys to his sister (not recorded).\n3. The man conveys to the friend (recorded on February 4).\n4. The sister conveys to the man (not recorded).\n5. The friend conveys to the farmer (recorded on February 5).\n\nGiven that the sister's conveyance to the man was not recorded, it does not affect the priority. The man's conveyance to the friend is recorded on February 4, and the farmer's conveyance to the man is recorded on February 5. Since the friend's conveyance is recorded after the farmer's conveyance, the friend's interest in the property is the highest priority.\n\nTherefore, the farmer, who purchased the property on February 5 without notice of the conveyance to the sister, would ultimately prevail.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "38eb5c9b5d831ea15e6e0874db7667584b4e555a60c7fe589be7356b404024a3", "gold": "A", "ts": 1790128880.8367994}
{"id": "mmlu/electrical_engineering/125", "group": "mmlu/electrical_engineering/125", "source": "mmlu", "kind": "mcq", "tier": "local", "model": "qwen2.5:3b-instruct", "correct": false, "y_error": 1, "features": {"req_tokens_log": 4.51085950651685, "req_messages": 1.0, "req_structured": 0.0, "req_tool": 0.0, "req_code": 0.0, "req_length": 0.0, "req_has_system": 0.0, "intent": "mcq", "data_class": "Public", "ret_top_sim": null, "ret_margin": null, "ret_coverage": null, "ret_verifier": null, "ret_sources": null, "ret_age_days_log": null, "ret_mismatch": null, "gen_mean_logprob": -0.36885549517527993, "gen_min_logprob": -2.0545220375061035, "gen_p10_logprob": -0.9702282547950745, "gen_mean_entropy": 0.7058504669670871, "gen_mean_margin": 4.43060120171594, "gen_frac_low_conf": 0.2485207100591716, "gen_stop_length": 0.0, "gen_out_tokens_log": 5.14166355650266, "gen_schema_valid": null, "gen_refusal": 0.0, "gen_hedge_rate": 0.0, "gen_truncated_ctx": 0.0, "gen_answer_marker": 1.0, "gen_self_contradiction": 0.0, "sys_queue_depth": 0.0, "sys_ttft_log": 3.924558230186716, "sys_mem_pressure": 0.13895100517032377}, "prompt_tokens": 90, "completion_tokens": 170, "ttft_ms": 49.63070600001629, "total_ms": 2518.821806999995, "finish_reason": "stop", "output": "Eddy current losses and hysteresis losses are both types of losses in electrical machines, but they are caused by different mechanisms. Eddy current losses occur in the core of the machine when an alternating magnetic field induces currents in the conductive core material. Hysteresis losses, on the other hand, are due to the hysteresis effect in the core material, which is the lagging of the magnetic flux behind the applied magnetic field. \n\nThin laminations are used to reduce eddy current losses by minimizing the size of the core material, thereby reducing the volume of material that can induce eddy currents. They do not directly affect hysteresis losses, which are more related to the material's magnetic properties and are reduced by using materials with lower hysteresis losses, such as silicon steel.\n\nAnswer: C", "output_sha256": "6e809fd9b482a8c8dd04fd83b2d3477b225e7652384b1eab09b586efc29236be", "gold": "A", "ts": 1790128882.1654506}
