e for the intended use, which would result in a loss for Beeson.\n\n4. **Sloan's Position**: Sloan argues for specific performance, claiming the doctrine of equitable conversion places the loss on the buyer (Beeson).\n\n5. **Doctrine of Equitable Conversion**: This doctrine generally applies when a party has suffered a loss due to the other party's breach of contract, and the non-breaching party would be unjustly enriched by enforcing the contract. However, it does not apply in this case because the loss is not due to the other party's breach but rather due to a third-party action (rezoning).\n\n6. **Analysis of Options**:\n   - **A. Sloan assumed the risk**: This is not applicable because the risk of rezoning was not assumed by Sloan.\n   - **B. Sloan would be unjustly enriched**: This is not applicable because the loss is due to the rezoning, not due to Sloan's actions.\n   - **C. Legal title remained in Sloan**: This is not applicable because the title would have transferred to Beeson upon payment of the balance.\n   - **D. Equity will relieve Beeson of a bad bargain**: This is the most relevant option. Beeson argues that enforcing the contract would be harsh and oppressive due to the rezoning, which makes the property unsuitable for the intended use.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "ebbd02e2ff2e379784a8dff185a287c411b6119824df6aa2453c8f0399cded0a", "gold": "B", "ts": 1790128894.7304058}
