\n\nA. The statutory provision is a form of unconstitutional prior restraint on a person's First Amendment right of free association.\n- This option is incorrect because the statute does not directly restrict free association but rather restricts a specific position within a union.\n\nB. Making it a crime for a Communist to hold a union office is a suspect classification, which violates the equal protection clause.\n- This option is incorrect because the statute does not involve classification based on race, gender, or other suspect classifications.\n\nC. The statutory prohibition is a reasonable method of discrimination since the benefit to the public outweighs the injury or restrictions that would be inflicted upon the person.\n- This option is incorrect because the statute does not provide a benefit to the public and is not reasonable in the context of prior restraint.\n\nD. The statutory provision in the act is a form of legislative punishment violative of the Constitution as a bill of attainder.\n- This option is correct because the statute directly declares someone guilty of a crime without a trial, which is a bill of attainder.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "d9636736a8cd079b3e02ec188cd7f901c39a39faf4790d7c42a4723754f273f4", "gold": "D", "ts": 1790128902.7344832}
