{"id": "mmlu/professional_law/459", "group": "mmlu/professional_law/459", "source": "mmlu", "kind": "mcq", "tier": "local", "model": "qwen2.5:3b-instruct", "correct": true, "y_error": 0, "features": {"req_tokens_log": 6.0867747269123065, "req_messages": 1.0, "req_structured": 0.0, "req_tool": 0.0, "req_code": 0.0, "req_length": 0.0, "req_has_system": 0.0, "intent": "mcq", "data_class": "Public", "ret_top_sim": null, "ret_margin": null, "ret_coverage": null, "ret_verifier": null, "ret_sources": null, "ret_age_days_log": null, "ret_mismatch": null, "gen_mean_logprob": -0.3083204583586677, "gen_min_logprob": -1.9835522174835205, "gen_p10_logprob": -1.0593658685684204, "gen_mean_entropy": 0.5617759573810176, "gen_mean_margin": 5.140434329969432, "gen_frac_low_conf": 0.18765432098765433, "gen_stop_length": 0.0, "gen_out_tokens_log": 6.008813185442595, "gen_schema_valid": null, "gen_refusal": 0.0, "gen_hedge_rate": 0.0, "gen_truncated_ctx": 0.0, "gen_answer_marker": 1.0, "gen_self_contradiction": 0.0, "sys_queue_depth": 0.0, "sys_ttft_log": 4.726436773691875, "sys_mem_pressure": 0.15408140116474134}, "prompt_tokens": 439, "completion_tokens": 406, "ttft_ms": 111.89258299998528, "total_ms": 5920.865886999991, "finish_reason": "stop", "output": "To answer this question, we need to consider the principles of notice and encumbrances under the title theory of mortgages and the notice recording statute.\n\n1. **Notice Recording Statute**: This statute requires that any encumbrances (like mortgages) be recorded to be effective against third parties. If a mortgage is not recorded, it does not affect the title of the property unless the third party is on constructive notice of the mortgage.\n\n2. **Title Theory of Mortgages**: Under this theory, a mortgage is a personal obligation of the mortgagor and does not affect the title of the property. The mortgage only binds the mortgagor and the mortgagee.\n\nGiven these points, let's analyze the options:\n\n- **Option A**: The rancher would be entitled to rescind the real estate contract with the florist immediately. This is incorrect because the encumbrance (mortgage) does not affect the title of the property, only the personal obligation of the mortgagor.\n- **Option B**: The encumbrance renders title to the western farm unmarketable. This is incorrect because the encumbrance does not affect the title of the property itself, only the personal obligation of the mortgagor.\n- **Option C**: The mortgage would only encumber the eastern farm, not the western farm, because the investor's partition of the property created a joint tenancy. This is incorrect because the partition did not affect the encumbrance of the mortgage.\n- **Option D**: The encumbrance would not entitle the rancher to rescind the real estate contract until closing on January 15. This is correct because the encumbrance only affects the personal obligation of the mortgagor and does not affect the title of the property. The rancher would not be entitled to rescind the contract until the encumbrance is known and could affect the sale, which would be after the closing date.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "b9ca1c290588c6d7edf72ff867e338d1d899d1f48f37c74c143bb9f75ad1d238", "gold": "D", "ts": 1790128912.1464398}
{"id": "mmlu/college_computer_science/36", "group": "mmlu/college_computer_science/36", "source": "mmlu", "kind": "mcq", "tier": "local", "model": "qwen2.5:3b-instruct", "correct": true, "y_error": 0, "features": {"req_tokens_log": 4.574710978503383, "req_messages": 1.0, "req_structured": 0.0, "req_tool": 0.0, "req_code": 0.0, "req_length": 0.0, "req_has_system": 0.0, "intent": "mcq", "data_class": "Public", "ret_top_sim": null, "ret_margin": null, "ret_coverage": null, "ret_verifier": null, "ret_sources": null, "ret_age_days_log": null, "ret_mismatch": null, "gen_mean_logprob": -0.3139349180880363, "gen_min_logprob": -1.9321337938308716, "gen_p10_logprob": -0.8637400269508362, "gen_mean_entropy": 0.5814284466722077, "gen_mean_margin": 4.4619098549089315, "gen_frac_low_conf": 0.1643835616438356, "gen_stop_length": 0.0, "gen_out_tokens_log": 5.683579767338681, "gen_schema_valid": null, "gen_refusal": 0.0, "gen_hedge_rate": 0.0, "gen_truncated_ctx": 0.0, "gen_answer_marker": 1.0, "gen_self_contradiction": 0.0, "sys_queue_depth": 0.0, "sys_ttft_log": 4.540651344657578, "sys_mem_pressure": 0.15408140116474134}, "prompt_tokens": 96, "completion_tokens": 293, "ttft_ms": 92.75184500006617, "total_ms": 4193.996577999997, "finish_reason": "stop", "output": "To determine which sorting algorithm has the least dependency on the initial ordering of the input, let's briefly consider each algorithm:\n\nA. **Insertion Sort**: This algorithm works by building a sorted list one item at a time. It is efficient when the input list is already partially sorted or nearly sorted. However, if the list is in reverse order or completely unsorted, it can perform poorly, with a worst-case time complexity of O(n^2).\n\nB. **Quicksort**: Quicksort typically has an average time complexity of O(n log n). However, its worst-case time complexity is O(n^2) when the input list is already sorted or nearly sorted. Quicksort's performance can degrade significantly if the pivot selection is poor.\n\nC. **Merge Sort**: This algorithm uses a divide-and-conquer approach to sort the list. It has a consistent time complexity of O(n log n) regardless of the initial ordering of the input. Merge sort is stable and does not depend on the initial order of the elements.\n\nD. **Selection Sort**: This algorithm sorts by repeatedly finding the minimum element from the unsorted part and putting it at the beginning. Its time complexity is O(n^2) in all cases, making it inefficient for large lists, regardless of the initial ordering.\n\nGiven these considerations, **Merge Sort** (C) is the algorithm that has the least dependency on the initial ordering of the input.\n\nAnswer: C", "output_sha256": "15daa36b2722486845be92f0e8ebfa9d8e06a841741f5ee614a7716e9dfa1641", "gold": "C", "ts": 1790128912.1498103}
