y, and without the owner's permission for the statutory period (10 years in this jurisdiction).\n\nIn this case, the woman had exclusive possession of the property from 1995 to 2005, which is more than the statutory period of 10 years. The son did not have any possession or use of the property during this period. Therefore, the son cannot claim adverse possession.\n\nNow, let's evaluate the options:\n\nA. The son will be unjustly enriched, inasmuch as the woman has paid all the taxes, insurance, and made necessary repairs.\n   - This is not relevant because the son cannot claim adverse possession.\n\nB. The son acquired his interest in the property by quitclaim deed.\n   - This is incorrect because the quitclaim deed did not create an interest in the property; it merely transferred the interest to the son.\n\nC. The deed from the husband to the son was void for lack of consideration.\n   - This is not relevant because the deed was valid and the son had a valid interest.\n\nD. The woman obtained the son's interest by adverse possession.\n   - This is incorrect because the son did not have any possession or use of the property during the 10-year adverse possession period.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "079749b4124fed384a4f1dcda0fcb0ca956fecc82e90210ec26379ad2b702a64", "gold": "D", "ts": 1790128940.2640483}
