ness and substantive due process. The tax itself does not seem to violate procedural fairness or substantive due process.\n\nC. The privileges and immunities clause of Article IV: This clause is concerned with the rights of citizens of one state to enjoy the privileges and immunities of citizens of other states. The commuter is not a resident of the city, so they do not have the privileges and immunities of a resident.\n\nD. The commerce clause: This clause is concerned with the regulation of interstate commerce. The tax does not seem to be regulating interstate commerce but rather the taxability of income derived from the city.\n\nGiven the commuter's status as a nonresident and the nature of the tax, the most applicable provision would be the privileges and immunities clause of Article IV, as it directly addresses the commuter's status and the tax's impact on them.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "3bf1b9d24c31ceb68e354e69fc628feef855be2c954560db8d42f396f94e00ec", "gold": "C", "ts": 1790128951.4352741}
