of disjoint paths, where each path has at least one vertex with no outgoing edges, but not necessarily every vertex.\n\n3. **Statement III: G has an isolated vertex, that is, one with neither an incoming edge nor an outgoing edge.**\n   - This is not necessarily true either. In a DAG, it is possible for every vertex to have at least one incoming and one outgoing edge, as long as the graph is acyclic. For example, a DAG could be a collection of disjoint paths, where each path has at least one vertex with no outgoing edges, but not necessarily every vertex.\n\nGiven the analysis, only Statement I must be true for a DAG with \\( |E| > 0 \\).\n\nAnswer: I", "output_sha256": "4030045e22dd099860e27c2f6fa36e227e419c368dc4a32811c16484ce989c4d", "gold": "D", "ts": 1790129036.5586576}
