 claim and the legal principle that such advice is crucial.\n\nOption B states that plea bargaining is not a critical necessity and counsel has no duty to discuss it. This contradicts the defendant's claim and the established legal principle.\n\nOption C suggests that there is no need for counsel to relay plea offers when the defendant wants to fight the charges. This is contrary to the defendant's claim and the legal principle that counsel must advise on all available options.\n\nOption D states that the defendant did not lose any rights and was not prejudiced by his lawyer's mistakes. This contradicts the defendant's claim and the legal principle that ineffective assistance of counsel can result in prejudice.\n\nAnswer: A", "output_sha256": "495eb7a760a8d080f1b0587475abe12b149c21056c8b65aa2d7f516226675f90", "gold": "A", "ts": 1790129045.0979617}
