tional, because it protects a legitimate state interest.\n- This option suggests that the statute is constitutional because it serves a legitimate state interest. However, the statute also affects interstate commerce, which could be seen as a legitimate state interest.\n\nB. Constitutional, because the commerce clause does not invalidate a state regulation relating to interstate commerce unless Congress takes express action to do so.\n- This option is plausible because the statute does not mention any express action by Congress to invalidate it. However, the statute still affects interstate commerce.\n\nC. Unconstitutional, because the state statute fails to give credit to the law of a neighboring state.\n- This option suggests that the statute is unconstitutional because it does not give credit to the neighboring state's law. However, the statute is still aimed at preventing evasion of state checkpoints, which is a legitimate state interest.\n\nD. Unconstitutional, because it unduly burdens interstate commerce.\n- This option suggests that the statute is unconstitutional because it unduly burdens interstate commerce. Given that the statute affects both state and interstate commerce, this could be a valid argument.\n\nConsidering the options, the most compelling reason to find the statute unconstitutional is that it unduly burdens interstate commerce, even though it serves a legitimate state interest.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "557f678cd33c5b4bb1bd6fededeaafdf7b553ac324ae62a676c49893ee915a5f", "gold": "A", "ts": 1790129045.8673837}
