 the 20-acre strip.\n\n3. **Condemnation of the Servient Tenement**: The railroad's easement is over the 20-acre strip, which is part of the 300-acre ranch. The rancher's death and the subsequent zoning of the ranch as residential property do not affect the railroad's easement over the strip. However, the state's power authority's condemnation of the 150 acres, including the 20-acre strip, extinguishes the easement.\n\nGiven these points, the railroad's easement is determinable and was terminated by the rancher's conveyance. Therefore, the railroad is not entitled to compensation when the servient tenement (the 300-acre ranch) is condemned.\n\nAnswer: D", "output_sha256": "4a87e4aa0acf1e3deb8403c7f9e0203152c82258d175aeadc0fc9778fbaf23e6", "gold": "C", "ts": 1790129069.4000456}
